task: channel-batch-floor (channel-chunk-batch-size, taken with the trade stated)
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-06
branch: feature/channel-batch-floor

why: Drain calls receiveMany(64) and gets ONE element back -- counted:
  block runs 4022 times for 4000 elements. Producer and consumer
  ping-pong, the ring never accumulates, and every handshake pays a
  Slot, an Async.Await, four Channel closures and a Drain copy: ~32%
  of the 672 bytes per element, plus a third of the interpreter steps.

the rule being reversed, on purpose and opt-in only: receiveMany's own
  doc says "batching RECEIVES delays nothing ... so this needs no
  flag". A batch FLOOR delays by design -- it holds the consumer until
  k elements are buffered -- so it is a flag, off by default, and the
  default path must not change by a byte.

mechanism: wake on a WATERMARK, not on every push. A floor receiver
  raises channel.floor = k and parks; the four push-side wake sites
  wake a receiver only when ring.size >= floor; close/fail wake
  unconditionally as today. WITHOUT a time bound this is a footgun
  (a live stream stalls at 5 of 16 forever), so the floor comes with a
  DWELL: Timer.after(dwell) wakes the receiver regardless, cancelled
  when the floor is met. Both surface as Drain parameters:
  c.drained(atLeast = 16, dwellMillis = 1).

what is measured: the elementwise channel lane, default against
  atLeast=16/dwell=1, by allocation count (immune to load) and by
  time if the box allows; plus the LATENCY side of the trade -- time to
  first element on a slow producer -- so the option is priced, not
  just sold.

hazard named: floor is one field per channel. Two consumers with
  different floors on one channel share it (last writer wins). Drain
  is per-consumer; a channel with several floor-drains is documented
  as unsupported rather than made to work here.
